  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 238 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nZonal flow aloft is expected to start this weekend, though an upper \nlevel trough will continue to track east across the western United \nStates. However, there remains some uncertainty in the forward speed \nof this trough, as well as its amplitude, which begins to throw a \nfair amount of uncertainty in the forecast for Sunday into the early \nweek. As for Saturday, expecting the zonal flow aloft to persist, \nkeeping relatively pleasant highs in the upper 70s across the \nregion. Throughout the day, expecting low chances for precipitation, \nthough chances for rain showers and a thunderstorm or two increase \nSaturday evening into the overnight hours. \n\nSunday, however, much will depend on where the trough eventually \nends up. Temperatures, for instance, remain highly uncertain, with \nlatest NBM guidance showing an astonishing 16 degree difference in \nthe 25th to 75th percentile solutions! Again, much depends on the \nlocation of the trough. If the trough arrives a bit more early, as \nsome solutions suggest, then we would favor the cooler temperatures \nand higher rain chances through the day, In this faster scenario, \nhighs would favor the mid 70s to lower 80s, with rain and \nthunderstorm chances in the afternoon. However, if the trough is \nslower tracking to the east, then we favor a warmer and potentially \ndrier solution. This scenario would favor highs in the lower 80s to \npossibly lower 90s across southwest Nebraska, with lower rain \nchances in the afternoon, potentially into the early evening. While \nuncertainty remains highest during the daytime hours, do see some \nslightly better agreement on the evening into the overnight hours, \nwhich should continue to favor rain chances overnight, especially \nacross northern Nebraska. This shows up well in ensemble guidance \nwith the European and GEFS ensembles both highlighting a 50 to 70 \npercent chance of exceeding one tenth of an inch across northern \nportions of Nebraska, with highest confidence in both runs along and \nnorth of Highway 20.\n\nGiven that solutions continue to struggle with the location of the \ntrough, expecting some uncertainty to remain into Monday, mostly in \nthe precipitation forecast, though there still remains some spread \nin the temperature forecast as well. However, given that the upper \nlevel trough is expected to track somewhere to the north, should see \nstronger winds aloft across portions of the region, but especially \nareas just east of the region. This may bring some stronger shear \nacross the region, and may be able to support some strong to \npotentially severe storms on Monday. Again, confidence remains on \nthe lower end, given the uncertainty in the location of the upper \nlevel trough. However, this may be a period of time worth keeping an \neye on, especially if the trough favors a slower track across the \nregion Sunday night into Monday afternoon .\n\nThe pattern is expected to remain somewhat active behind the system \non Monday, with another trough pushing into the region. This should \ncontinue to favor continuing chances for rain and thunderstorms \nthrough mid week, with highs generally remaining in the 70s, though \nsome cooler solutions suggest highs only in the 60s for Tuesday. If \nthese cooler temperatures come to fruition, this would be slightly \ncooler than seasonal, as our typically highs for mid September are \nright around 80 to 81 degrees. However, with the fairly active \npattern and high uncertainty in this weekend, still have medium \nlevels of uncertainty in the mid week forecast, as large scale \nmodels continue to be slightly out of sync on timing of these \npotential upper level systems.",
